#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-reset(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:7
msgid "git-reset - Reset current HEAD to the specified state"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:15
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit reset [-q] [<tree-ish>] [--] <pathspec>...\n"
"\tgit reset [-q] [--pathspec-from-file=<file> [--pathspec-file-nul]] "
"[<tree-ish>]\n"
"\tgit reset (--patch | -p) [<tree-ish>] [--] [<pathspec>...]\n"
"\tgit reset [--soft | --mixed [-N] | --hard | --merge | --keep] [-q] "
"[<commit>]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:22
msgid ""
"In the first three forms, copy entries from _<tree-ish>_ to the index.  In "
"the last form, set the current branch head (`HEAD`) to _<commit>_, "
"optionally modifying index and working tree to match.  The "
"_<tree-ish>_/_<commit>_ defaults to `HEAD` in all forms."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git reset [-q] [<tree-ish>] [--] <pathspec>...`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:24
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`git reset [-q] [--pathspec-from-file=<file> [--pathspec-file-nul]] "
"[<tree-ish>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:28
msgid ""
"These forms reset the index entries for all paths that match the "
"_<pathspec>_ to their state at _<tree-ish>_.  (It does not affect the "
"working tree or the current branch.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:32
msgid ""
"This means that `git reset <pathspec>` is the opposite of `git add "
"<pathspec>`. This command is equivalent to `git restore "
"[--source=<tree-ish>] --staged <pathspec>...`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:39
msgid ""
"After running `git reset <pathspec>` to update the index entry, you can use "
"linkgit:git-restore[1] to check the contents out of the index to the working "
"tree. Alternatively, using linkgit:git-restore[1] and specifying a commit "
"with `--source`, you can copy the contents of a path out of a commit to the "
"index and to the working tree in one go."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:40
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git reset (--patch | -p) [<tree-ish>] [--] [<pathspec>...]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:44
msgid ""
"Interactively select hunks in the difference between the index and "
"_<tree-ish>_ (defaults to `HEAD`).  The chosen hunks are applied in reverse "
"to the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:48
msgid ""
"This means that `git reset -p` is the opposite of `git add -p`, i.e.  you "
"can use it to selectively reset hunks. See the \"Interactive Mode\" section "
"of linkgit:git-add[1] to learn how to operate the `--patch` mode."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "`git reset [<mode>] [<commit>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:55
msgid ""
"This form resets the current branch head to _<commit>_ and possibly updates "
"the index (resetting it to the tree of _<commit>_) and the working tree "
"depending on _<mode>_. Before the operation, `ORIG_HEAD` is set to the tip "
"of the current branch. If _<mode>_ is omitted, defaults to `--mixed`. The "
"_<mode>_ must be one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:57
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--soft`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:62
msgid ""
"Does not touch the index file or the working tree at all (but resets the "
"head to _<commit>_, just like all modes do). This leaves all your changed "
"files \"Changes to be committed\", as `git status` would put it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:63
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--mixed`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:67
msgid ""
"Resets the index but not the working tree (i.e., the changed files are "
"preserved but not marked for commit) and reports what has not been "
"updated. This is the default action."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:70
msgid ""
"If `-N` is specified, removed paths are marked as intent-to-add (see "
"linkgit:git-add[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--hard`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:75
msgid ""
"Resets the index and working tree. Any changes to tracked files in the "
"working tree since _<commit>_ are discarded.  Any untracked files or "
"directories in the way of writing any tracked files are simply deleted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--merge`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:83
msgid ""
"Resets the index and updates the files in the working tree that are "
"different between _<commit>_ and `HEAD`, but keeps those which are different "
"between the index and working tree (i.e. which have changes which have not "
"been added).  If a file that is different between _<commit>_ and the index "
"has unstaged changes, reset is aborted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:86
msgid ""
"In other words, `--merge` does something like a `git read-tree -u -m "
"<commit>`, but carries forward unmerged index entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--keep`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:92
msgid ""
"Resets index entries and updates files in the working tree that are "
"different between _<commit>_ and `HEAD`.  If a file that is different "
"between _<commit>_ and `HEAD` has local changes, reset is aborted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:93
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--recurse-submodules`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-recurse-submodules`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:99
msgid ""
"When the working tree is updated, using `--recurse-submodules` will also "
"recursively reset the working tree of all active submodules according to the "
"commit recorded in the superproject, also setting the submodules' `HEAD` to "
"be detached at that commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:103
msgid ""
"See \"Reset, restore and revert\" in linkgit:git[1] for the differences "
"between the three commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:106
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:108
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:111
msgid "Be quiet, only report errors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--refresh`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:113
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-refresh`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:115
msgid "Refresh the index after a mixed reset. Enabled by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:116
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pathspec-from-file=<file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:123
msgid ""
"Pathspec is passed in _<file>_ instead of commandline args. If _<file>_ is "
"exactly `-` then standard input is used. Pathspec elements are separated by "
"_LF_ or _CR_/_LF_. Pathspec elements can be quoted as explained for the "
"configuration variable `core.quotePath` (see linkgit:git-config[1]). See "
"also `--pathspec-file-nul` and global `--literal-pathspecs`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:124
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pathspec-file-nul`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:128
msgid ""
"Only meaningful with `--pathspec-from-file`. Pathspec elements are separated "
"with _NUL_ character and all other characters are taken literally (including "
"newlines and quotes)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:133
msgid "Do not interpret any more arguments as options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:134
#, no-wrap
msgid "`<pathspec>...`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:136
msgid "Limits the paths affected by the operation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:138
msgid "For more details, see the 'pathspec' entry in linkgit:gitglossary[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo add"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:150
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ edit                                     <1>\n"
"$ git add frotz.c filfre.c\n"
"$ mailx                                    <2>\n"
"$ git reset                                <3>\n"
"$ git pull git://info.example.com/ nitfol  <4>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:156
msgid ""
"You are happily working on something, and find the changes in these files "
"are in good order.  You do not want to see them when you run `git diff`, "
"because you plan to work on other files and changes with these files are "
"distracting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:157
msgid "Somebody asks you to pull, and the changes sound worthy of merging."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:162
msgid ""
"However, you already dirtied the index (i.e. your index does not match the "
"`HEAD` commit).  But you know the pull you are going to make does not affect "
"`frotz.c` or `filfre.c`, so you revert the index changes for these two "
"files.  Your changes in working tree remain there."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:164
msgid ""
"Then you can pull and merge, leaving `frotz.c` and `filfre.c` changes still "
"in the working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:165
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo a commit and redo"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git commit ...\n"
"$ git reset --soft HEAD^      <1>\n"
"$ edit                        <2>\n"
"$ git commit -a -c ORIG_HEAD  <3>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:177
msgid ""
"This is most often done when you remembered what you just committed is "
"incomplete, or you misspelled your commit message, or both.  Leaves working "
"tree as it was before \"reset\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:178
msgid "Make corrections to working tree files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:181
msgid ""
"\"reset\" copies the old head to `.git/ORIG_HEAD`; redo the commit by "
"starting with its log message.  If you do not need to edit the message "
"further, you can give `-C` option instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:183
msgid "See also the `--amend` option to linkgit:git-commit[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:184
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo a commit, making it a topic branch"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:190
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git branch topic/wip          <1>\n"
"$ git reset --hard HEAD~3       <2>\n"
"$ git switch topic/wip          <3>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:196
msgid ""
"You have made some commits, but realize they were premature to be in the "
"`master` branch.  You want to continue polishing them in a topic branch, so "
"create `topic/wip` branch off of the current `HEAD`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:197
msgid "Rewind the master branch to get rid of those three commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:198
msgid "Switch to `topic/wip` branch and keep working."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo commits permanently"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git commit ...\n"
"$ git reset --hard HEAD~3   <1>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:211
msgid ""
"The last three commits (`HEAD`, `HEAD^`, and `HEAD~2`) were bad and you do "
"not want to ever see them again.  Do *not* do this if you have already given "
"these commits to somebody else.  (See the \"RECOVERING FROM UPSTREAM "
"REBASE\" section in linkgit:git-rebase[1] for the implications of doing so.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:212
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo a merge or pull"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:224
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git pull                         <1>\n"
"Auto-merging nitfol\n"
"CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in nitfol\n"
"Automatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result.\n"
"$ git reset --hard                 <2>\n"
"$ git pull . topic/branch          <3>\n"
"Updating from 41223... to 13134...\n"
"Fast-forward\n"
"$ git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD       <4>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:229
msgid ""
"Try to update from the upstream resulted in a lot of conflicts; you were not "
"ready to spend a lot of time merging right now, so you decide to do that "
"later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:232
msgid ""
"\"pull\" has not made merge commit, so `git reset --hard` which is a synonym "
"for `git reset --hard HEAD` clears the mess from the index file and the "
"working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:234
msgid ""
"Merge a topic branch into the current branch, which resulted in a "
"fast-forward."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:239
msgid ""
"But you decided that the topic branch is not ready for public consumption "
"yet.  \"pull\" or \"merge\" always leaves the original tip of the current "
"branch in `ORIG_HEAD`, so resetting hard to it brings your index file and "
"the working tree back to that state, and resets the tip of the branch to "
"that commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:240
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo a merge or pull inside a dirty working tree"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:249
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git pull                         <1>\n"
"Auto-merging nitfol\n"
"Merge made by recursive.\n"
" nitfol                |   20 +++++----\n"
" ...\n"
"$ git reset --merge ORIG_HEAD      <2>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:255
msgid ""
"Even if you may have local modifications in your working tree, you can "
"safely say `git pull` when you know that the change in the other branch does "
"not overlap with them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:260
msgid ""
"After inspecting the result of the merge, you may find that the change in "
"the other branch is unsatisfactory.  Running `git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD` "
"will let you go back to where you were, but it will discard your local "
"changes, which you do not want.  `git reset --merge` keeps your local "
"changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:262
#, no-wrap
msgid "Interrupted workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:268
msgid ""
"Suppose you are interrupted by an urgent fix request while you are in the "
"middle of a large change.  The files in your working tree are not in any "
"shape to be committed yet, but you need to get to the other branch for a "
"quick bugfix."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:279
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git switch feature  ;# you were working in \"feature\" branch and\n"
"$ work work work      ;# got interrupted\n"
"$ git commit -a -m \"snapshot WIP\"                 <1>\n"
"$ git switch master\n"
"$ fix fix fix\n"
"$ git commit ;# commit with real log\n"
"$ git switch feature\n"
"$ git reset --soft HEAD^ ;# go back to WIP state  <2>\n"
"$ git reset                                       <3>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:282
msgid "This commit will get blown away so a throw-away log message is OK."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:284
msgid ""
"This removes the 'WIP' commit from the commit history, and sets your working "
"tree to the state just before you made that snapshot."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:287
msgid ""
"At this point the index file still has all the WIP changes you committed as "
"'snapshot WIP'.  This updates the index to show your WIP files as "
"uncommitted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:289
msgid "See also linkgit:git-stash[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:290
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reset a single file in the index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:295
msgid ""
"Suppose you have added a file to your index, but later decide you do not "
"want to add it to your commit. You can remove the file from the index while "
"keeping your changes with git reset."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:300
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git reset -- frotz.c                      <1>\n"
"$ git commit -m \"Commit files in index\"     <2>\n"
"$ git add frotz.c                           <3>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:304
msgid ""
"This removes the file from the index while keeping it in the working "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:305
msgid "This commits all other changes in the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:306
msgid "Adds the file to the index again."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:307
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keep changes in working tree while discarding some previous commits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:314
msgid ""
"Suppose you are working on something and you commit it, and then you "
"continue working a bit more, but now you think that what you have in your "
"working tree should be in another branch that has nothing to do with what "
"you committed previously. You can start a new branch and reset it while "
"keeping the changes in your working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:323
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git tag start\n"
"$ git switch -c branch1\n"
"$ edit\n"
"$ git commit ...                            <1>\n"
"$ edit\n"
"$ git switch -c branch2                     <2>\n"
"$ git reset --keep start                    <3>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:326
msgid "This commits your first edits in `branch1`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:330
msgid ""
"In the ideal world, you could have realized that the earlier commit did not "
"belong to the new topic when you created and switched to `branch2` "
"(i.e. `git switch -c branch2 start`), but nobody is perfect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:332
msgid ""
"But you can use `reset --keep` to remove the unwanted commit after you "
"switched to `branch2`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:333
#, no-wrap
msgid "Split a commit apart into a sequence of commits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:341
msgid ""
"Suppose that you have created lots of logically separate changes and "
"committed them together. Then, later you decide that it might be better to "
"have each logical chunk associated with its own commit. You can use git "
"reset to rewind history without changing the contents of your local files, "
"and then successively use `git add -p` to interactively select which hunks "
"to include into each commit, using `git commit -c` to pre-populate the "
"commit message."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:351
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git reset -N HEAD^                        <1>\n"
"$ git add -p                                <2>\n"
"$ git diff --cached                         <3>\n"
"$ git commit -c HEAD@{1}                    <4>\n"
"...                                         <5>\n"
"$ git add ...                               <6>\n"
"$ git diff --cached                         <7>\n"
"$ git commit ...                            <8>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:357
msgid ""
"First, reset the history back one commit so that we remove the original "
"commit, but leave the working tree with all the changes. The `-N` ensures "
"that any new files added with `HEAD` are still marked so that `git add -p` "
"will find them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:361
msgid ""
"Next, we interactively select diff hunks to add using the `git add -p` "
"facility. This will ask you about each diff hunk in sequence and you can use "
"simple commands such as \"yes, include this\", \"No don't include this\" or "
"even the very powerful \"edit\" facility."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:365
msgid ""
"Once satisfied with the hunks you want to include, you should verify what "
"has been prepared for the first commit by using `git diff --cached`. This "
"shows all the changes that have been moved into the index and are about to "
"be committed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:372
msgid ""
"Next, commit the changes stored in the index. The `-c` option specifies to "
"pre-populate the commit message from the original message that you started "
"with in the first commit. This is helpful to avoid retyping it. The "
"`HEAD@{1}` is a special notation for the commit that `HEAD` used to be at "
"prior to the original reset commit (1 change ago).  See "
"linkgit:git-reflog[1] for more details. You may also use any other valid "
"commit reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:374
msgid ""
"You can repeat steps 2-4 multiple times to break the original code into any "
"number of commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:377
msgid ""
"Now you've split out many of the changes into their own commits, and might "
"no longer use the patch mode of `git add`, in order to select all remaining "
"uncommitted changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:380
msgid ""
"Once again, check to verify that you've included what you want to. You may "
"also wish to verify that git diff doesn't show any remaining changes to be "
"committed later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:381
msgid "And finally create the final commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:384
#, no-wrap
msgid "DISCUSSION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:387
msgid "The tables below show what happens when running:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:390
#, no-wrap
msgid "git reset --option target\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:394
msgid ""
"to reset the `HEAD` to another commit (`target`) with the different reset "
"options depending on the state of the files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:403
msgid ""
"In these tables, `A`, `B`, `C` and `D` are some different states of a "
"file. For example, the first line of the first table means that if a file is "
"in state `A` in the working tree, in state `B` in the index, in state `C` in "
"`HEAD` and in state `D` in the target, then `git reset --soft target` will "
"leave the file in the working tree in state `A` and in the index in state "
"`B`.  It resets (i.e. moves) the `HEAD` (i.e. the tip of the current branch, "
"if you are on one) to `target` (which has the file in state `D`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:412
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" A       B     C    D     --soft   A       B     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  A       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   D       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge (disallowed)\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep  (disallowed)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:422
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" A       B     C    C     --soft   A       B     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  A       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   C       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge (disallowed)\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep   A       C     C\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" B       B     C    D     --soft   B       B     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  B       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   D       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge  D       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep  (disallowed)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:442
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" B       B     C    C     --soft   B       B     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  B       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   C       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge  C       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep   B       C     C\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:452
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" B       C     C    D     --soft   B       C     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  B       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   D       D     D\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge (disallowed)\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep  (disallowed)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:462
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" B       C     C    C     --soft   B       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  B       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   C       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge  B       C     C\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep   B       C     C\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:472
msgid ""
"`git reset --merge` is meant to be used when resetting out of a conflicted "
"merge. Any mergy operation guarantees that the working tree file that is "
"involved in the merge does not have a local change with respect to the index "
"before it starts, and that it writes the result out to the working tree. So "
"if we see some difference between the index and the target and also between "
"the index and the working tree, then it means that we are not resetting out "
"from a state that a mergy operation left after failing with a conflict. That "
"is why we disallow `--merge` option in this case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:481
msgid ""
"`git reset --keep` is meant to be used when removing some of the last "
"commits in the current branch while keeping changes in the working tree. If "
"there could be conflicts between the changes in the commit we want to remove "
"and the changes in the working tree we want to keep, the reset is "
"disallowed. That's why it is disallowed if there are both changes between "
"the working tree and `HEAD`, and between `HEAD` and the target. To be safe, "
"it is also disallowed when there are unmerged entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:484
msgid "The following tables show what happens when there are unmerged entries:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" X       U     A    B     --soft  (disallowed)\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  X       B     B\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   B       B     B\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge  B       B     B\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep  (disallowed)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block .
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:503
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"working index HEAD target         working index HEAD\n"
"----------------------------------------------------\n"
" X       U     A    A     --soft  (disallowed)\n"
"\t\t\t  --mixed  X       A     A\n"
"\t\t\t  --hard   A       A     A\n"
"\t\t\t  --merge  A       A     A\n"
"\t\t\t  --keep  (disallowed)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-reset.adoc:506
msgid "`X` means any state and `U` means an unmerged index."
msgstr ""

